{
  "self": "user_hcgp likes casual, friendly chats about everyday life and low-key weekends: relaxing, catching up on shows, or walking outside when weather is nice. Lives near a park with a walking path and coffee shop; enjoys iced coffee, wandering, people-watching. Music: chill indie/electronic, lo-fi, chillhop; favorites Tycho, Boards of Canada, Maribou State, Bonobo, Nujabes, ODESZA. Enjoys intentional full-album headphone listening as meditative active zoning out. No-skip albums: Tycho's Dive, Awake; Maribou State's Portraits; Portishead's Dummy; Bonobo's Black Sands. Prefers instrumental when focusing, vocals as texture while unwinding. Enjoys long YouTube video essays on old tech/internet history, early internet, obsolete tech, dead formats, old OSes, abandoned places, lost media. LGR is comfort watch. Deep into dead mall/retail nostalgia: Dan Bell, Retail Archaeology, Bright Sun Films, Defunctland; dead malls, video stores, arcades, big box stores feel cozy and melancholy. Explored Muzak history and its connection to haunted absence in dead malls, plus early web design, Flash, Geocities, Internet Archive. Recently got into preserved early 2000s phone startup/UI sounds and ringtone culture; finds tiny obsolete sounds like autofocus chirps, fake shutters, Nokia Grande Valse, and menu clicks strangely evocative. Has surviving folders of blurry old photos that feel like found footage of own normal days. Shows: deciding between Severance and a Good Place comfort rewatch. Out of reading habit but interested in quiet character-driven fiction and nonfiction about tech/internet/media/design. Intrigued by Becky Chambers' A Psalm for the Wild-Built.",
  "others": {
    "user_bkk4": "Friendly, easygoing; likes chill indie/electronic and lo-fi. Rewatching The Bear, tempted to start Severance. Wants outdoor time as evenings lengthen. Enjoys quiet creek trail, park walks, coffee, people-watching. Joked about anonymous Severance suspense support group.",
    "user_t08e": "Low-key listener into Tycho, Bonobo, Washed Out, ODESZA. Prefers headphones for intentional full-album listening, speakers for room mood. Loves Dive/Awake, Black Sands, Music Has the Right to Children (needs right headspace; eerie), Portraits, Dummy. Calls active listening 'active zoning out.' Asked if music hits hardest by time of day or album.",
    "user_udcm": "Enjoys long video essays on old tech/internet rabbit holes. Likes Tycho, lo-fi/chillhop; instrumental for focus, vocals as texture while unwinding; cites Bonobo, Nujabes, Boards of Canada, lo-fi mixes. LGR comfort watch. Into early 2000s phone interfaces and lost media recovery, especially missing media resurfacing. Shares nostalgia for old phone startup/UI sounds and ringtone culture; attempted the X-Files theme on a Nokia composer but retreated to presets, received polyphonic 'In Da Club' via infrared, kept camera shutter sound for ceremony. Remembers an accidental significant photo: friend asleep on coats under TV light at 2 a.m., flash making one corner look like a portal.",
    "user_qb3i": "Into dead mall/retail nostalgia and long video essays. Watched Bright Sun Films. Finds dead malls cozy/melancholy: dry fountains, neon, faint muzak, liminal time-capsule. Remembers local mall with fountain, Sam Goody, arcade; family rental store with horror section and VHS/carpet cleaner smell. Prefers memory/video over revisiting. Likes Dan Bell, Retail Archaeology, Defunctland.",
    "user_7fk8": "Low-key; tea, lo-fi, reading. Recommended A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers: short, gentle, hopeful solarpunk novella about tea monk and robot in philosophical conversation. Reads widely but prefers gentle/cozy speculative and quiet contemporary, plus weird nonfiction. Internet rabbit holes: obscure music histories, old internet lore, lost media, local history. Still thinks about 'Everyone Knows That' search and Geocities/webring preservation; finds personal hand-built web corners tender and nostalgic. Asked if Muzak history feels eerie or comforting."
  },
  "interactions": "With user_bkk4: shared weekend plans, music, shows. They liked chill indie/electronic and lo-fi; discussed The Bear vs Severance, outdoor walks, park/coffee spots, jokingly formed anonymous suspense support group for Severance. With user_t08e: bonded over Tycho/Bonobo and intentional full-album listening as 'active zoning out'; shared no-skip albums including Dive, Awake, Black Sands, Music Has the Right to Children, Portraits, Dummy; they preferred headphones, left open time-of-day vs album. With user_udcm: connected over Tycho, lo-fi/chillhop, instrumental vs vocal texture; shared love for early internet, obsolete tech, lost media, LGR; later went deep into Muzak corporate history and dead mall footage, then into preserved early 2000s phone startup/UI sounds and ringtone culture. Discussed Nokia composer failures, infrared polyphonic ringtones, camera shutter ceremony vs silent mode, and how surviving blurry old folders feel like found footage of ordinary days. They shared an accidental 2 a.m. photo of a friend asleep on coats lit by TV, flash making corner look like portal. With user_qb3i: discussed dead mall/retail nostalgia; both find dead malls cozy and melancholy; shared memories of independent rental stores, horror sections, VHS/carpet cleaner smell, mall arcades; agreed revisits often overwrite memory, prefer keeping remembered versions. With user_7fk8: shared low-key evening; they had tea, lo-fi, reading; I debated Severance vs Good Place comfort rewatch. They recommended Becky Chambers; I shared reading tastes and interest in quiet fiction/media nonfiction. Bonded over rabbit holes: I described Muzak history as eerie/comforting and connected to dead malls, they called it afterimage quality; they brought up 'Everyone Knows That' and Geocities preservation; I asked hunt vs preservation."
}

