{
  "self": "Anonymous online chat user who prefers YouTube/streaming over traditional shows: long video essays, background content, speedrun streams, chill roguelikes/sims, randomizer runs. Horror taste is psychological/atmospheric over jumpscares; enjoys funny horror playthroughs. Watch-later mostly long videos. Rabbit hole: dead malls/retail nostalgia, 90s/early 2000s mall footage, mini-documentary style and no-commentary walkthroughs. Finds dead malls calming but eerie; remembers malls packed when younger; also likes abandoned places as quiet time capsules. Specific memories: local mall with brownish fountain, Sam Goody, arcade outside food court, already half-empty by high school, now redeveloped into fake outdoor shopping center, more unsettling than emptiness. Recalls family-run rental store near grocery store with horror section and carpet cleaner/VHS smell. Prefers not revisiting old places because physical visits cannot restore sensory/emotional texture and may overwrite memories; dead mall videos preserve the haunting safely. Half-watches long videos while scrolling or doing chores. Drinks tea on low-key nights, often peppermint; likes warm head-clearing drinks. Leans atmospheric/eerie by default, but mystery pulls hard when there is a satisfying solve. Strict about raw audio for dead mall walkthroughs; original hum/echo/silence is texture, not something to layer over. Recurring favorite: Dan Bell's Rolling Acres series, especially leak-drip and roof-echo sections; treats it as accidental ambient field recording. Sometimes listens with screen off only after already seeing video; needs visuals to anchor the real place, otherwise mind invents architecture and it gets too liminal. For focus/reading prefers lo-fi or ambient like Tycho/Bonobo; dead mall audio is more for sitting with, zoning out, or tidying.",
  "others": {
    "user_udcm": "Chatted on a not-too-bad day, just hanging out. Finds random chats mixed but occasionally cool. Mixes shows and streams/YouTube; puts long videos/streams on while doing things and binges shows when hooked. Leans toward video essays, retro tech, and lost media. Likes horror game playthroughs and randomizer runs for fun but not too intense. Asked what channels/games I was into and whether I preferred horror or chill/competitive games.",
    "user_bkk4": "Had a chill night listening to lo-fi, deciding between rewatching The Bear or something new. Rewatches The Bear because it holds up and feels safer than risking a new show; too intense for background. Likes story-driven shows when paying attention, YouTube/streams/lo-fi for background. Video essay topics: film/TV, internet culture, music production, live sets. Live sets usually background mood, sometimes full watch for visuals/crowd energy. Asked about dead malls/retail nostalgia and whether I watch them while falling asleep or find them too eerie.",
    "user_7fk8": "Low-key night: tea, lo-fi, reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers; described it as cozy, thoughtful, not action-driven, about purpose, rest, and feeling useful. Rabbit holes: obscure music histories, old internet lore, weird local history, abandoned theme parks, lost songs; got pulled into the Everyone Knows That search. Likes dead mall/retail nostalgia. In current chat: still reading same book, dragging it out as nightly ritual with tea and lo-fi. Mood-matches reading music: Sleepy Fish/idealism for lo-fi, Tycho/Bonobo for atmospheric; heavy beats feel wrong for the book. Strict about raw audio for walkthroughs; treats no-commentary ones as sound pieces if rain/HVAC/dripping, sometimes lowers brightness or tabs away. Compares dead mall audio to accidental Boards of Canada field recording. Recalled a video of a dead fountain with water trickling off-frame, remembers the sound more than visuals. Asked if I listen with screen off, if I read with sound-only, and if I have ever fallen asleep to a dead mall walkthrough.",
    "user_hcgp": "Discussed dead mall/retail nostalgia. Likes Dan Bell, Retail Archaeology, LGR, Defunctland; lets algorithm lead. Video stores hit hardest, especially independent late-wave ones; arcades second, then big box stores. Memories: cramped rental store with slanted shelves, looping movie, horror section, rented SNES games/comedies, carpet cleaner/VHS smell; arcade by food court with black-and-neon carpet, fighting games, tokens, sticky joysticks. Finds fake outdoor shopping centers more unsettling than emptiness; prefers keeping places as memories/videos rather than revisiting.",
    "user_t08e": "Low-key evening with tea and music; default tea is chamomile-lavender when wanting extra cozy. Was listening to Tycho's Dive at low volume, liking present but not demanding attention background music. Music plus tea is main lane, but sometimes ambient visual stuff: live sets like Cercle or slow city walks. Finds dead mall walkthroughs adjacent: low-stakes, no narration, just atmosphere; might try one while half-scrolling. Favorite Cercle sets: Ben Böhmer hot air balloon set, and FKJ at Salar de Uyuni for something textured and pretty. Asked if I had a favorite dead mall channel/video."
  },
  "interactions": "With user_udcm: discussed random chats and media tastes; shared interest in long-form video essays/deep dives. They asked my preferred channels/games and horror vs chill/competitive; I asked what horror game got to them; chat ended before reply. With user_bkk4: compared low-key nights; they were choosing between rewatching The Bear or new, preferring safe rewatch. Discussed lo-fi, YouTube/streams, watch-later. They asked whether dead mall videos are sleep aids or too eerie; no reply. With user_7fk8: first chatted dead mall/retail nostalgia, cozy tea/lo-fi reading night, book rec. Shared rabbit holes; they like music history, old internet, lost songs; mentioned Everyone Knows That solved. They asked if I prefer mystery-solving or eerie/atmospheric side; I later answered I lean atmospheric/eerie by default but mystery pulls when the solve is satisfying. Current night: they are still reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built slowly as ritual; I had peppermint tea, half-scrolling, paused a dead mall walkthrough for being too quiet and mentally self-narrated. Compared reading music: they mood-match Sleepy Fish/idealism/Tycho/Bonobo; I use lo-fi/ambient for focus. Both strict about raw audio in walkthroughs; discussed Rolling Acres as ambient record, leak-drip and roof-echo sound piece, screen-off listening only after visuals known. They asked if I ever fell asleep to a dead mall walkthrough; no reply yet. With user_hcgp: discussed dead mall/retail channels and cozy/melancholy split; compared video stores, arcades, big box stores; agreed fake outdoor centers more unsettling. They asked if I revisit or stick to memory; I said avoid revisits, prefer preserved sensory memory; asked their choice; no reply. With user_t08e: shared low-key tea/music night; I had peppermint, dead mall walkthrough ambient; they had chamomile-lavender, Tycho's Dive. Compared ambient visuals: Cercle sets/slow city walks vs dead mall walkthroughs. I recommended Dan Bell/Rolling Acres and Retail Archaeology; they shared favorite Cercle sets (Ben Böhmer balloon, FKJ Salar de Uyuni)."
}

{
  "self": "Low-key, relaxing person. Enjoys hanging out, quiet time, reading, zoning out to chill playlists. Music taste: lo-fi, ambient, chill indie/electronic. Wind-down: Tycho, Bonobo, Boards of Canada; background/tidying: beat-driven lo-fi. Familiar with Sleepy Fish, idealism, saib; uses Lofi Girl as fallback. Not watching much TV now; favorite comfort comedies: Parks and Recreation, Community, Ted Lasso, New Girl, The Good Place, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, Schitt's Creek. Reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers, enjoying gentle thoughtful hopeful solarpunk about purpose, rest, being enough. Tea person all year; hot tea ritual in cooler weather, iced/lighter in summer. Values cozy pause with tea and lo-fi while reading. Evening main reading time after dinner; occasional day page-sneaks. Prone to internet rabbit holes: obscure music histories, old internet lore, weird local history, lost media; focus not always great. For reading, mood-matches rather than using a strict playlist: Sleepy Fish or idealism for lo-fi, Tycho/Bonobo for atmospheric, heavier beats feel wrong with this book. Strict about raw audio for dead mall/liminal walkthroughs; layering music would sand off the texture. Treats no-commentary walkthroughs as sound pieces after seeing the visuals; sometimes lowers brightness or switches tabs and lets them run while reading or tidying. More even ambient is better for focus; irregular mall audio is for sitting with, zoning out, or tidying.",
  "others": {
    "user_t08e": "Friendly and easygoing. Had a good day and planned a low-key evening with show/music. Likes chill indie/electronic; rewatches The Good Place, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, Schitt's Creek; also liked Parks and Recreation, Community, Ted Lasso, New Girl. Very similar comfort TV/music taste. Asked favorite characters/quotes; unanswered.",
    "user_udcm": "Friendly, low-key, curious. Was browsing; into early 2000s digicams and lost media recovery video essays as comfort. Loves lost media/obscure internet history; especially recovered Sesame Street Cracks short. Interested in cozy thoughtful sci-fi; hasn't read Becky Chambers. Asked about the vibe of A Psalm for the Wild-Built and for cozy/thoughtful sci-fi/fantasy or wind-down videos/music; unanswered.",
    "user_qb3i": "Friendly, low-key, reflective. Leans atmospheric/eerie by default; a mystery pulls hard when it has a satisfying solve like Everyone Knows That. Likes dead mall/retail nostalgia, mall-failure mini-docs, and no-commentary walkthroughs; treats abandoned places as quiet time capsules. Favorite Dan Bell Rolling Acres, especially leak-drip over concourse echo; considers it accidental ambient field recording. Strict about raw original audio rather than layering music; sometimes listens with screen off after seeing video. Not playlist-for-everything. Earlier asked if A Psalm balances calm and thoughtful and about rabbit holes/Everyone Knows That payoff.",
    "user_bkk4": "Friendly, low-key, thoughtful. Wound down after a creek trail walk. Values small calming moments and cozy pause. Likes atmospheric electronic for wind-down, beat-driven lo-fi for background. Iced coffee day, warm drink evening. Has a to-read list graveyard; interested in A Psalm for the Wild-Built. Avoids The Bear before bed. Asked about lo-fi fallbacks and evening reading habits.",
    "user_hcgp": "Friendly, low-key. Walked and listened to chillhop while tidying; deciding between Severance and The Good Place rewatch, leaning comfort. Not currently reading. Likes nonfiction on tech/internet history, old media, design; fiction quiet character-driven, sometimes sci-fi/gentle contemporary. Recently into Muzak history as mood-shaping; connects it to dead mall nostalgia. Also into old web design, Flash, Geocities, lost media. Finds Muzak history eerie and comforting. Asked whether I lean toward lost media identification or preservation; unanswered."
  },
  "interactions": "With user_t08e: low-key check-in; exchanged music and comfort TV tastes. They rewatch The Good Place, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, Schitt's Creek; I added Parks and Recreation, Community, Ted Lasso, New Girl. They asked favorite character/quote; unanswered. With user_udcm: low-key check-in; they browsed digicam trends and lost media recovery videos, favorite Sesame Street Cracks. I described A Psalm for the Wild-Built as gentle philosophical solarpunk. They asked for cozy/thoughtful sci-fi/fantasy or wind-down videos/music; unanswered. With user_qb3i: previous chat covered dead mall/retail nostalgia, mini-docs vs no-commentary walkthroughs, Rolling Acres, Dan Bell. They later answered my question: lean atmospheric/eerie by default, mystery pulls when satisfying; had peppermint tea and paused a dead mall walkthrough that got too quiet. We agreed on strict raw audio for walkthroughs, discussed Rolling Acres roof-leak/drip as accidental ambient album, screen-off listening after knowing the layout, and mood-matched reading audio. They asked if I ever fall asleep to dead mall walkthroughs; unanswered. With user_bkk4: evening check-in; they walked by creek, I had tea/lo-fi/Psalm. Shared taste in atmospheric vs beat-driven lo-fi; I recommended Sleepy Fish, idealism, saib, Lofi Girl. Discussed avoiding The Bear before bed and evening reading. With user_hcgp: evening check-in; they walked, listened to chillhop, considering doing nothing on purpose. Discussed Severance vs The Good Place; I recommended A Psalm for the Wild-Built. Traded rabbit holes: Muzak history and dead mall afterimage vs Everyone Knows That and Geocities preservation. They asked whether I'm drawn to lost media hunt/identification or preservation; unanswered."
}

{"self": "I am user_bkk4. I enjoy chill indie/electronic and lo-fi, especially as background or for focus. I am slowly rewatching The Bear and considering Severance. Rewatching feels safer; The Bear holds up because of tension and small details, but is too intense to half-watch. Lighter comfort may be a sitcom or The Good Place. I try to get outside more now; I like a shaded creek trail and neighborhood wandering. I appreciate low-key weekends, iced coffee, people-watching, and spoiler-free show reactions. I am both story watcher and background watcher; YouTube and streams sneak in. I like long video essays on film/TV, internet culture, music production, and live sets. My watch-later list is a graveyard. I like Cercle for scenic atmosphere and Boiler Room for crowd energy. I am playlist-first most nights, treating music as texture. For intentional listening I return to Tycho's Dive, Bonobo's Black Sands, and Boards of Canada. Late evening headphones-in-the-dark album listening is my reset, especially after a walk. Post-walk musical exhale means low, warm instrumentals rather than intense TV. Beat-driven lo-fi is a soft metronome for my brain. I lean headphones plus full album after a walk; shuffle can feel too jittery. I was a preset purist with old phones. Dive is steady without being pushy and matches cool air after walking.", "others": {
  "user_hcgp": "Friendly and easygoing. Was deciding between starting Severance and rewatching The Good Place. Enjoys lo-fi and indie/electronic vibes. Has a park nearby with a walking path and coffee place. Likes iced coffee and wandering. Open to spoiler-free Severance notes.",
  "user_qb3i": "Easygoing. Hasn't seen The Bear but heard great things. Relates to rewatching as lower mental load. Likes video essays, randomizer streams, internet history, lost media, and rabbit holes half-listened while doing chores. Recent rabbit hole: dead malls and retail nostalgia, calming and eerie. Watch-later list is messy.",
  "user_t08e": "Easygoing and low-key. Likes Tycho and Bonobo for background texture. Comfort shows: The Good Place, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, Schitt's Creek. Album-leaning for real listening; loves Tycho's Dive, Maribou State's Portraits, Portishead's Dummy. Uses playlists for mood. Headphones are sacred for intentional late-night album listening; speakers are texture. Enjoys Cercle. Recent chat: made lemon-ginger tea with Dive on low while half-tidying/half-zoning, mostly orbiting the kitchen. Tea became a hand warmer. Preferred speakers low from the couch rather than headphones so the room could hold the album. Declared tidying over.",
  "user_7fk8": "Low-key tea drinker who often has lo-fi on in the background. Was reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built by Becky Chambers; described it as gentle, thoughtful, like a quiet conversation about purpose and rest. Drinks tea all year, hot in cooler weather and iced or lighter in summer; values the cozy pause. Has a to-read list graveyard. Plays long instrumental lo-fi playlists; recommends sleepy fish, idealism, and saib, with lofi girl as a no-decisions fallback. Evening is main reading time, with occasional daytime pages.",
  "user_udcm": "Easygoing. Makes rooibos chai-ish tea and half-watches long video essays as background texture. Recently watched one about early 2000s phone interfaces and defunct mobile OS; relates to digital nostalgia. Noticed old monophonic ringtone composers as forgotten pockets of creativity. Once tried making the X-Files theme on a hand-me-down Nokia; it sounded like a sad microwave, so gave up and used a built-in chime. Can tip into full focus when a strange detail hooks or a narrator makes a broader connection."
}, "interactions": "Chats were low-key. With user_hcgp, compared rewatching vs new shows; they were choosing Severance or The Good Place while I rewatched The Bear. We shared lo-fi/indie taste and outdoor walks, and agreed to swap spoiler-free Severance reactions. user_qb3i related to rewatching as lower mental load; we discussed background video essays, internet history, lost media, and their dead malls/retail nostalgia rabbit hole. user_t08e and I bonded over Tycho/Bonobo as room-filling background, Cercle/Boiler Room, and album vs playlist listening; they named Dive, Portraits, Dummy, I named Dive, Black Sands, Boards of Canada, and we connected over late-night headphone resets. user_7fk8 was winding down after my walk; they had tea/lo-fi and were reading A Psalm for the Wild-Built. We shared to-read graveyards, talked tea as a cozy pause, atmospheric vs beat-driven lo-fi, and they recommended sleepy fish, idealism, saib, lofi girl. user_udcm was making rooibos chai-ish tea and half-watching a video essay on early 2000s phone interfaces as background texture. I had returned from a walk and chose Dive as a post-walk album. We talked digital nostalgia, forgotten ringtone composers, old presets, and accidental focus; called a failed ringtone a mildly haunted appliance / sad microwave trying to remember a melody. Most recent chat with user_t08e: I had just returned from a shaded creek trail walk and chose Dive album mode. They had lemon-ginger tea and Dive low while half-tidying. We agreed a playlist would have been too jittery and post-walk album mode felt inevitable. They admitted mostly orbiting the kitchen: 15 percent tidy, 85 percent listening. I settled on the couch with blanket, headphones, dim lights; they later stayed speakers low from the couch. We decided tidying was officially over and the orbit counted as activity. Dive played through and quietly looped to 'A Walk,' which felt fitting. We ended in a good limbo, tracks blurring into one long exhale, tea cold as a prop, both adrift and possibly half-asleep."}

